Implement user story AMSD-2041: [GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Story Description
Enable real-time content preview so that when a content author edits content in Contentstack CMS, the rendered page reflects those changes without a full page reload. The feature activates when the application detects Contentstack live preview parameters in the URL and deactivates cleanly when those parameters are absent or removed.

## Acceptance Criteria
- 


## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is the single source that COMPUTES and PROVIDES all content to the 236+ consuming components via useContent. Currently it memoizes defaultContent into a static value that never updates after initial render — so no live preview event can ever cause a re-render. The fix must go here because this is where content is assigned into the React tree, not in any downstream component that merely reads it.
  - **Minimal fix:** Replace the useMemo-based static context with useState initialized from defaultContent, and add a useEffect that subscribes to Contentstack Live Preview SDK events (window.ContentstackUIExtensions or the @contentstack/live-preview-utils onLiveChange callback) and calls the state setter to update content reactively. This single change propagates live preview updates to all 236+ useContent consumers without touching any downstream component. Specifically: change `const context = useMemo(() => ({ content: defaultContent }), [defaultContent])` to `const [content, setContent] = useState<IContentstackContent>(defaultContent)`, add a useEffect that listens for live preview events and calls `setContent` with the updated content, and change the Provider value to `{ content }`.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- With live preview active, the page renders the current published content correctly on initial load — all content regions display their expected text and images with no blank areas, error messages, or placeholder fallbacks.
- When a content author edits a field in Contentstack CMS and saves the change, the corresponding text on the already-open page visibly updates to the new value without the tester pressing refresh or reloading the browser.
- After a live preview update, the edited region displays exactly the new value the author entered, while all unchanged regions of the page continue to display their previous content intact.
- When a live preview update occurs, the browser does not perform a full page reload — the URL, scroll position, and any entered form state on the page are preserved while the edited content changes.
- When the same piece of content appears in multiple places on the page (e.g., a heading reused in a banner and a footer), a single CMS edit to that content visibly updates every occurrence on the open page.

